CPU ref  : results_gpudyn3_linux_2026-06-11.log (same day, same host/inputs)

WHAT CHANGED (G0 was wiring, as predicted by the strategy doc)
  GPU2/Makefile           : + mpi-gpu target -- the `gpu' object list
    (gpunb.velocity.cu + gpupot.gpu.cu CUDA libs, SSE gpuirr, cnbint) with
    the standalone main swapped for the MPI driver + real MPI wrapper,
    exactly as mpi-cpu vs cpu. Fully additive; no shared-target edits.
  GPU2/Makefile_cpu.build : + mpi-gpu link target (gpu link options:
    -lstdc++ -fopenmp -lcudart; FC arrives as mpif90).
  GPU2/lib/gpunb.velocity.cu : GPUNB_devinit now binds each MPI rank to ONE
    device, devid[local_rank % numGPU] (launcher env detection --
    OMPI_COMM_WORLD_LOCAL_RANK / MPI_LOCALRANKID / SLURM_LOCALID -- same
    idea as the gpuirr.cpp rank-0 I/O guard; opt-out NBODY_GPU_RANK_BIND=0;
    GPU_LIST still defines the pool). No launcher env -> path unchanged.
    Covers gpupot.gpu.cu too (it inherits the device via gpunb_devinit_()).
    Side benefit: pure-MPI (OMP_NUM_THREADS=1) on a multi-GPU node would
    previously have died on assert(numGPU <= numCPU).
  run_equiv.sh / profile_scaling.sh : wrap.sh now execs the actual
    $(basename $BIN) instead of the hardcoded mpi-cpu name, so BIN=...
    overrides test any variant.
  NOTE gpuirr.sse.cpp needs NO rank-0 guard: it writes no files (NBSTAT is
    a gpuirr.cpp-only diagnostic), stderr stays live by design.

